// Kaiju: original stories written for this game. Format: docs/v3/BLUEPRINT-FORMAT.md.

== kaiju.f01 | feature | three-act
title: The Catfish Under Edo
genre: kaiju + historical-drama
tone: Vast, earthy and blackly comic
era: 1855 | period
setting: The low city of Edo, its carpenters' quarter and its pleasure district | real | city
logline: In the month when the gods are away, the giant catfish that lies under Japan slips the stone that pins it and rises through the shogun's capital, and a woodblock printer's apprentice discovers that half the city hates the monster and the other half is getting rich from it.
premise: Everyone knows the earth is carried on the back of a great catfish, held still by a god with a stone. In the tenth month the gods go to a gathering in the west. Two years ago black ships forced the harbour; since then prices have risen and the rich have hoarded. {okiku}, seventeen, cuts blocks for a publisher of cheap prints. The night the ground heaves, she is the one who sees what comes up out of the river.
synopsis: The first act is the rising. A night of small tremors; wells go muddy; eels climb out of the moats. Then the river stands up. The creature is not malevolent: it is an animal the size of a district, rolling over, and the city is on top of it. Whole wards go down. Fire follows. {okiku} drags her master {old_tsuta} out of the workshop with the blocks still in his arms. The second act is the weeks the catfish lies in the ruins, half in the river, breathing, too large to move and too large to kill. The government posts notices that nothing has occurred. The samurai fire cannon at it to no effect. And the city, being a city, adapts. Carpenters, plasterers and timber merchants have never earned so much; coins the rich had buried come flying out of their storehouses, and in the pleasure quarter labourers are spending like lords. {okiku} cuts the first print of the catfish overnight, unlicensed and anonymous. It sells out. She cuts another: the catfish scolded by a mob. Another: the catfish as a rich man's nightmare, making him vomit gold for the poor. Four hundred designs in two months, hers and everyone's, faster than censors can read. Her friend {shinza}, a carpenter with new money, toasts the fish. Her mother died under a beam. She feels both. A magistrate, {inspector_goto}, comes to seize the blocks and cannot find the publisher because there is none. In the third act the gods come home. The film stages it without embarrassment: a figure with a great gourd-shaped stone walking up the river at dawn, seen by a girl, a drunk and a child. The catfish is pressed down, not slain; it will always be there. The prints are banned the same week and the blocks are burned in a heap. {okiku} keeps one impression: the fish and the townspeople holding it down together, some with ropes and some with coins. She pastes it inside a cupboard.
question: What does a disaster reveal about who was already being crushed?
themes: catastrophe as redistribution; satire as a pressure valve; living on top of what you cannot kill
audience: Adults and teenagers; monster-film fans who want folklore and wit
viewpoint: With {okiku}, at street level; the creature is always too big for the frame
stakes: A city's survival, a workshop's livelihood, and the right to draw what everyone saw
conflict: A city against an animal that is also its foundation, and a printmaker against a government that says nothing happened
ending: The god returns and pins the catfish under the city again. The prints are burned. One survives in a cupboard.
experience: Awe at scale, dark laughter, the thrill of an underground press, and an ending that settles without resolving
look: Woodblock colour and line brought to life; lantern-lit ruins against a mountainous wet flank; paper prints fluttering everywhere
sound: A groan below hearing, roof tiles sliding, a carpenters' work song. Baren on paper, fast.
m: understand | creature | order-vs-change | restored
@ okiku | Okiku | lead | protagonist | human | she/her | 17 | To draw what she saw, and sell it | Her mother is under the house and her hands need to do something | It is unlicensed, and it is true | Quick, sardonic, grief held at arm's length | From apprentice to the hand behind a city's laughter | Downtown slang, fast | Indigo apron, a cutting knife in her hair
@ old_tsuta | Old Tsuta | supporting | mentor | human | he/him | 66 | His blocks, his shop, no trouble | Forty years of pear wood | The apprentice is braver than he is | Fussy, timid, proud of her | From forbidding it to pulling the proofs himself | Complains continuously | Spectacles tied on with string, cherry-wood blocks in his arms
@ shinza | Shinza | co-lead | foil | human | he/him | 22 | Work, wages, a good time | He has never had money before | His luck is other people's loss | Open-handed, loud, decent | From toasting the fish to helping hold it down | Carpenter's banter | Headband, a saw, a new silk lining to his coat
@ inspector_goto | Inspector Goto | supporting | antagonist | human | he/him | 45 | The prints stopped | Officially nothing has happened | There is no publisher to arrest | Stiff, harassed, privately amused | From seizures to buying one for himself | Edicts, sighs | Two swords, a satchel of confiscated paper
@ the_catfish | The Catfish | featured | none | monster | it | - | To turn over | It has been pinned for a long time | A city is built on it | Indifferent, immense | Pressed down again | A groan below hearing | A flank like a hillside, whiskers like fallen masts
~ okiku - old_tsuta | apprentice and master | he forbids it | he inks the block
~ okiku - shinza | printmaker and carpenter | she resents his luck | they pull on the same rope
~ okiku - inspector_goto | anonymous hand and censor | a search | a print bought under the counter
